 "deeper": [
  {
   "title": "Why Bell's palsy closes nothing but a stroke closes the eye",
   "html": "<p>The facial nucleus part for the upper face receives fibres from both hemispheres, while the lower face part receives only the opposite side. A stroke cuts only one side's input, so the forehead is still driven by the other hemisphere and the patient can wrinkle it and close the eye. A lesion of the facial nerve itself cuts everything below it, so the whole half of the face falls. That one difference tells a stroke apart from Bell's palsy.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's facial nerve slides on bilateral and contralateral input.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why sympathetic fibres reach the head from the chest",
   "html": "<p>Sympathetic outflow only leaves the cord between T1 and L2, yet the eye, face and sweat glands of the head are supplied too. The fibres climb up the sympathetic chain to the superior cervical ganglion, then ride on the carotid arteries into the head. A lesion anywhere along that long route, including a lung apex tumour, can cause Horner's syndrome.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's sympathetic anatomy and Horner's syndrome slides.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why diabetic autonomic neuropathy makes you dizzy on standing",
   "html": "<p>Standing pools blood in the legs, and normally the sympathetic system constricts vessels at once to hold blood pressure. When diabetes damages those autonomic fibres, the reflex fails, pressure drops on standing and the patient feels faint. The same damage explains the bladder and sexual symptoms the lecture lists.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's clinical correlations slide.</p>"
  },
  {
   "title": "Why a herniated disc causes pain down the leg",
   "html": "<p>The sciatic nerve is built from the L4 to S3 roots. A disc bulging at the lower lumbar levels presses on one of those roots before it even joins the nerve, and the brain reads the pain as coming from wherever that root supplies, down the back of the thigh and into the leg. That is why the pain of sciatica follows a dermatome rather than staying in the back.</p><p class='src'>Source: the lecture's sciatic nerve and sciatica slides.</p>"
  }
 ],
